  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The next round of rain showers is expected for \nthe end of the week, though drought relief is unlikely. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure over the mid-Atlantic region results in dry \nconditions until Friday. Low pressure to the northwest in Canada\ntracks eastward, bringing a relatively weak cold front over the\nstate Friday into Saturday. Pressure gradient between the high \npressure and the approaching low could help produce some breezy \nsouthwest winds Thursday afternoon. \n\nBased on global models, PWATs are roughly 1.25-1.75 inches, \nwith higher values concentrated around the central and Downeast \nregions. Models continue to hint at precipitation generally \nmoving in early Friday from the northwest, and moving to the \nsoutheast by Friday night. There is still some model \ndiscrepancies in the end timing, however. Global ensembles \ngenerally keep precipitation totals between half an inch to an\ninch. Some members, though, do show a narrow band of enhanced \nprecipitation, upwards of an inch, somewhere located around\nCentral Highlands and Downeast region.",
